      <td>30</td>
    </tr>
    <!-- Add more rows as needed -->
  </tbody>
</table>
```

JavaScript:
```javascript
function sortTable(n) {
  var table, rows, switching, i, x, y, shouldSwitch, dir, switchcount = 0;
  table = document.getElementById("myTable");
  switching = true;
  dir = "asc";
  while (switching) {
    switching = false;
    rows = table.rows;
    for (i = 1; i < (rows.length - 1); i++) {
      shouldSwitch = false;
      x = rows[i].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
      y = rows[i + 1].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
      if (dir == "asc") {
        if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() > y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
          shouldSwitch = true;
          dir = "desc";
        }
      } else if (dir == "desc") {
        if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() < y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
          shouldSwitch = true;
          dir = "asc";
        }
      }
      if (shouldSwitch) {
        rows[i].parentNode.insertBefore(rows[i + 1], rows[i]);
        switching = true;
        switchcount ++;
      }
    }
  }
  if (switchcount == 0 && dir == "asc") {
    dir = "desc";
    for (i = 0; i < rows.length; i++) {
      rows[i].className += " reverse";
    }
  }
}
```

In this example, the table is sorted by clicking the headers. The `sortTable` function takes an argument `n` which represents the column index to sort by. The table rows are swapped if the current row's content is greater than the next row's content (ascending) or if the current row's content is less than the next row's content (descending).

The CSS class `reverse` is used to change the sorting direction when the table is sorted in descending order. You can add this class to the `<tr>` elements in the CSS file:

```css
tr.reverse {
  background-color: #f5f5f5;
}
```

This example sorts the table alphanumerically, but you can modify the comparison logic in the JavaScript code to sort the table based on your specific requirements.

