To implement a multiple-choice dialogue in a text-based Java game, you can use a loop to present the choices to the player and handle their selection. Here's a simple example:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class MultipleChoiceDialogue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] choices = {"Option 1", "Option 2", "Option 3"};
        int playerChoice = -1;

        System.out.println("You see three options:");
        for (int i = 0; i < choices.length; i++) {
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + choices[i]);
        }

        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        while (playerChoice < 0 || playerChoice > choices.length) {
            System.out.print("Enter your choice (1-" + choices.length + "): ");
            playerChoice = scanner.nextInt();
        }

        System.out.println("You chose: " + choices[playerChoice - 1]);
    }
}
```

In this example, the `choices` array contains the options presented to the player. The `playerChoice` variable stores the player's selection. The loop displays the choices to the player and takes their input. The while loop ensures that the player's input is within the valid range.

You can customize this code to fit your specific game requirements, such as adding more options, displaying the choices differently, or handling the player's choice differently based on the context of the game.

